# Routing table for .strm / remote-URL sources.
#
#   <url-prefix>  [302|parallel]        one prefix per line, mode optional
#   <key> = <value>                     global tuning settings
#   # ...                               comment, to end of line
#
# Matching is StartsWith, case-insensitive, against MediaSourceInfo.Path.
# Omitting the mode means 302. The mode decides which patch handles the source:
#
#   302        the server answers a redirect and steps out of the transfer path.
#              Right when the origin does NOT throttle a single connection.
#   parallel   the server fetches the file over several concurrent Range requests
#              and relays it. Right when the origin throttles PER CONNECTION —
#              a 302 would just move that bottleneck to the player.
#              Run parallel/probe-origin.sh first; do not guess.
#
# Both modes disable transcoding for the matched source: once ffmpeg re-encodes,
# the point of either mode is gone.
#
# A misspelled mode voids the whole line and is reported by `embypatch check` —
# it is never quietly downgraded to 302, because "parallel is on but somehow
# still stuttering" is a miserable thing to debug.
#
# Changes take effect within 30 seconds — no server restart needed.
# An empty file means every patch is inert and the server behaves exactly like stock Emby.
#
# Install to <programdata>/config/strm-routing.txt
# Or override with EMBY_STRM_PREFIXES="https://a/ parallel;https://b/ 302"
# Or point at any path with EMBY_STRM_CONFIG=/path/to/strm-routing.txt
#
# Keep prefixes narrow. Anything else Emby serves over http (Live TV channels, for example)
# must not match, or it will lose its transcoding fallback.

https://pan.example.com/                parallel
https://fast-cdn.example.net/media/     302
https://other.example/                             # no token = 302

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Global settings. Priority is environment variable > this file > built-in default.
# Keys are case-insensitive and '_' is the same as '-'.
# They live here rather than in bin/emby-server because an Emby upgrade rewrites
# that file and leaves programdata/ alone — tuning that vanishes on upgrade shows
# up months later as "it started stuttering again" and nobody suspects the knobs.
#
#   ramp-seconds            6      EMBY_STRM_RAMP_SECONDS          seconds between added connections
#   connections             6      EMBY_STRM_CONNECTIONS           concurrent Range requests, per stream
#   max-origin-connections 12      EMBY_STRM_MAX_ORIGIN_CONNECTIONS  cap across ALL streams, per origin
#   chunk-mb                8      EMBY_STRM_CHUNK_MB              bytes per Range request
#   buffer-mb             128      EMBY_STRM_BUFFER_MB             reorder buffer ceiling
#   initial-connections     2      EMBY_STRM_INITIAL_CONNECTIONS   connections opened immediately
#   log                   off      EMBY_STRM_LOG                   diagnostics file path
#
# connections is per STREAM; max-origin-connections is what the ORIGIN sees, across every
# stream in the process. Seeking makes the two disagree — abandoning a stream does not free
# its connections at the origin instantly — so the second number is the one that keeps a burst
# of seeks, or a third viewer, from crossing whatever cliff your origin has. Set it below
# connections and the fetcher lowers connections to match, and says so.
#
# ramp-seconds is the one worth tuning per origin, and it has a cliff at 1.
# Sweep it against your own origin before changing it (run-tests.sh seeks 25),
# and only after parallel/probe-origin.sh says your origin throttles per connection.

# ramp-seconds = 2
# log = /var/packages/EmbyServer/target/programdata/logs/strm-parallel.log
